# Eisenhower National Historic Site (the farms west of the Emmitsburg Road, Cumberland Township, Confederate ground of July 2 and 3): three National Park Service reports
#   NPS-EISE-CLR1  Cultural Landscape Report for Eisenhower NHS, Volume 1 (Olmsted Center for Landscape Preservation; Stakely, Coffin Brown, Killion; ed. Hegeman), PDF p. 41 (chapter 1, 'Civil War and effects, 1860-1899')
#   NPS-EISE-CLI-REDDING-2004  Cultural Landscapes Inventory: Clement Redding Farm (the farm of David Sandoe in 1863)
#   NPS-EISE-CLI-LANDSCAPE  Cultural Landscapes Inventory: Eisenhower NHS Landscape (Farms #1, #2 and #3)
# The farms 'escaped the brunt of the battle' (the reports' words); they were Confederate camp and line ground. The reports say what the owners' records and damage claims say; they hold no field-by-field map of 1863.
# Farm numbers: Farm #1 the Eisenhower farm (Biesecker, tenant Bollinger), Farm #2 (Douglass), Farm #3 (Pitzer), the Clement Redding Farm (Sandoe from 1861).
# The text layer of the Olmsted Center report keeps the fi and fl ligature letters as single code points; cleaned quotes below are typed with the plain letters and match through the letters and digits.
@ NPS-EISE-CLR1

41/17 | general | Owners and tenants of the Eisenhower farms when the war began | - | 1860-1863 | stated | The report says that at the outbreak of the Civil War Farm #1 was owned by John Biesecker with Adam Bollinger as tenant, Farm #2 by William Douglass, Farm #3 by Samuel Pitzer and the future Clement Redding Farm by David Sandoe | #s1-2
41/17 | damage | All the Eisenhower farms were damaged by artillery, trampling and looting and occasionally used as first aid stations and hospitals | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says the farms escaped the brunt of the battle because the main skirmishes were to the east, but all were damaged by artillery, trampling and looting and were occasionally used as first aid stations and hospitals | =During the Battle of Gettysburg on July 1-4, 1863, these farms escaped the brunt of the battle, as the main skirmishes occurred to the east. However, all of the farms were subject to damage caused by artillery, trampling, and looting. They were also occasionally used as first aid stations and hospitals.
41/17 | hospitals | A double log barn on Farm #1 may have served as an aid station for wounded Confederates on the second day | - | 1863-07-02 | hedged | The report says one of the double log barns on Farm #1 may have served as an aid station for wounded Confederates during the second day of the battle | =One of the double log barns located on Farm #1 may have served as such an aid station for wounded Confederates during the second day of the battle.
41/17 | earthworks | Confederate line of defense along the eastern boundaries of Farms #1, #2 and #3; breastworks across the east and south boundaries of Farms #1 and #2 | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says Confederate forces established a continuous line of defense along the eastern boundaries of Farms #1, #2 and #3 and that breastworks (temporary low defensive walls) were installed across the east and south boundaries of Farms #1 and #2 (a figure on the page shows the line of breastworks) | =Confederate forces established a continuous line of defense along the eastern boundaries of Farms #1, #2, and #3. Breastworks (temporary low defensive walls) were installed across the east and south boundaries of Farms #1 and #2.
41/17 | fences | A stone wall along the eastern border of the Clement Redding Farm may date from the battle period; no evidence it was used for cover | - | 1863 (a wall that the report says may date to this period) | hedged | The report says a stone wall along the eastern border of the Clement Redding Farm may date to the period of the battle but that there is no evidence it was used for cover during the battle | =A stone wall located along the eastern border of the Clement Redding Farm may date to this period, but there is no evidence that it was used for cover during the battle.
41/17 | general | The Eisenhower farms as Confederate campsites and supply-train ground; the Pitzer Schoolhouse as General Longstreet's headquarters | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says Farms #1, #2 and #3 were used as campsites for Confederate units and supply trains and the Pitzer Schoolhouse was used as the headquarters for General Longstreet during the battle | =In addition to providing a line of defense, Farms #1, #2, and #3 were used as campsites for Confederate units and supply trains and the Pitzer Schoolhouse was used as the headquarters for General Longstreet during the battle.
38/14 | buildings | Farm #1 in 1863: owned by the Bieseckers, occupied by the tenants Bollinger | - | 1863-07 | stated | The report says the Bieseckers returned to their previous home in 1860 and leased the farm (later the Eisenhower farm) to Adam Bollinger and his wife, and that in 1863 at the time of the battle the Bieseckers still owned the property and the Bollingers occupied it as resident tenants | They continued to farm the land for nine years >>> as resident tenants.
38/14 | buildings | Farm #1 log house in the mid-1800s: brick addition, brick facing on the log walls, summer kitchen with a large brick bake oven | - | ~mid-1800s (renovation of the original log house; the report does not say whether before or after 1863) | stated | The report says that during the mid-1800s the original log house was updated with a brick addition on the south side and brick facing on all exterior log walls, and a summer kitchen with a large brick bake oven was built south of the house about the same time | During the mid-1800s the original log house >>> large brick bake oven was included as part of this structure.
38/14 | buildings | Farm #1 when the Armstrong interest was transferred to McMordie: two two-story log houses, two double log barns, a log spring house | - | ~1825 (state at a transfer of the 1820s; the report does not say how many of these buildings stood in 1863) | stated | The report says that at the transfer to McMordie the 320-acre parcel included two two-story log houses, two double log barns and a log spring house | At the time of the transfer to McMordie >>> and a log spring house.
38/14 | buildings | Farm #2 at the death of Isaac Armstrong in 1835: a stone house, a double log barn, a large orchard, a tanyard | - | ~1835 (state at the owner's death; the report does not say what stood in 1863) | stated | The report says that by Isaac Armstrong's death in 1835 his farm, later Eisenhower Farm #2, was the site of a prosperous tannery and, a 150-acre farm, was improved with a large two-story stone house, a double log barn, a large orchard, a tanyard with seventeen vats, a bark shed, a bark mill and a shop | #s24-25
39/15 | buildings | Farm #3: a stone house built by the Pitzers; Pitzer's schoolhouse built in the mid-1850s and sold to the school system in 1857 | - | pre-1863 (the Pitzer stone house and the schoolhouse of the 1850s) | stated | The report says the Pitzer family constructed a stone house on the farm, that Samuel Pitzer built a small schoolhouse on its southwest corner in the mid-1850s, and that Pitzer's School House was sold to the public school system in 1857 | #s16-19
43/19 | buildings | A log house and barn on a twenty-acre tract of the Pitzer farm rented by John Flaharty in the early 1860s | - | ~1860-1863 (rented in the early 1860s) | stated | The report says that in the early 1860s John Flaharty was renting a log house and barn on a twenty-acre tract at the southeastern corner of the Pitzer holdings, separated from the main farm by Millerstown Road | #s1-2
43/19 | buildings | David Sandoe bought the Clement Redding farm in 1861; the large bank barn dates from after the mid-1860s | - | 1861 (purchase) and after the mid-1860s (bank barn) | stated | The report says David Sandoe purchased the farm in 1861 and lived and worked on it for forty years, and that the large bank barn, the major addition of his tenure, was constructed sometime between the mid-1860s and 1885 | #s13-14

@ NPS-EISE-CLI-REDDING-2004

24/22 | damage | The Clement Redding Farm was hit by artillery fire and lost farm resources to trampling and looting | - | 1863-07 | stated | The inventory says that David Sandoe bought the farm in 1861 and that, while Farms #1, #2 and #3 escaped the most intense fighting in 1863, the Clement Redding Farm was subjected to artillery fire and to losses of farm resources through trampling and looting by the soldiers | #s5-6
24/22 | earthworks | Breastworks across the east and south perimeters of Farms #1 and #2; a stone wall on the east border of the Clement Redding Farm that may date to the Civil War | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The inventory says breastworks extended across the east and south perimeters of Farms #1 and #2 and that a stone wall along the eastern border of the Clement Redding Farm may date to the Civil War era although there is no conclusive evidence that it was used for cover during battle | #s7-8
24/22 | damage | After the war all the Eisenhower farms except the Sandoes' filed claims for livestock, acres of crops, fencing and damage to land and gardens | - | ~1863-1870s (claims after the battle) | stated | The inventory says that occupants of all the farms of the Eisenhower site filed reimbursement claims except the Sandoes of the Clement Redding Farm, and that the claims recorded loss of livestock, acres of crops, clothing, foodstuff, equipment, fencing and damage to farm land and gardens | #s11-12
34/32 | orchards | A Warren survey of 1868 shows an orchard to the west of the Clement Redding farm; scattered fruit trees today | - | ~1868 (Warren survey, five years after the battle) | stated | The inventory says scattered fruit trees in the fields northeast of the house indicate the possibility of orchards and that the Warren survey of 1868 illustrated an orchard to the west | #s14
34/32 | woodlots | A stretch of mature hickories along Willoughby's Run and Red Rock Road suggested by the park historian to have been the farm woodlot | - | ~historic farm woodlot (period not stated; the park historian's suggestion) | hedged | The inventory says a vegetated stretch along Willoughby's Run and Red Rock Road with several very mature hickory trees and no understory has been suggested by the park historian to have historically been the farm woodlot | #s16-18
34/32 | fields | Crop fields and pastures around the building clusters since at least the mid-nineteenth century | - | 1850s onward (at least since the mid-nineteenth century) | stated | The inventory says the crop fields and pastures surrounding the building clusters have existed since at least the mid-nineteenth century | #s19

@ NPS-EISE-CLI-LANDSCAPE

33/31 | earthworks | The Confederate defensive line along the woodlots and the eastern boundary of Farms #1, #2 and #3; Longstreet's headquarters | - | 1863-07 | stated | The inventory says the Confederate defensive line formed a continuous line along the woodlots and the eastern boundary of Farms #1, #2 and #3, that the farms were used as campsites for several Confederate units and supply trains, and that Farm #3 served as the headquarters of General Longstreet | #s24-26
33/31 | damage | All the farms of the site were subjected to artillery fire and losses to trampling and looting; owners in 1860-65 | - | 1860-1865 | stated | The inventory says that just before the Civil War Farm #1 was owned by the Bieseckers, Farm #2 by W. Douglass, Farm #3 by Samuel Pitzer and the Clement Redding Farm by the Sandoe family, and that all the farms, though they escaped the most intense fighting, were subjected to artillery fire and to losses of farm resources through trampling and looting | #s22-23
